%I A402157 #10 Nov 22 2026 17:25:35
%S A402157 1,8,20,24,27,32,44,48,54,55,56,64,80,84,92,104,110,116,117,120,123,
%T A402157 128,132,140,144,145,147,159,160,164,168,171,176,184,188,195,200,203,
%U A402157 204,208,212,216,220,224,225,234,246,248,252,253,260,264,272,275,288,290
%N A402157 Numbers k such that A061026(k) < A034694(k): the smallest m with k | phi(m) is less than the smallest prime == 1 (mod k).
%C A402157 Equivalently, the numbers k for which A061026(k) is not prime. To see this, first note that A061026(k) <= A034694(k): for p = A034694(k), k divides phi(p) = p - 1. Now suppose A061026(k) = q is prime. Then k divides q - 1, so q >= A034694(k), and therefore q = A034694(k).
%C A402157 Erdős remarked that this sequence is easily shown to be infinite. He also conjectured that A034694(k)/A061026(k) tends to infinity for almost all k, which would imply that the sequence has asymptotic density 1 (Erdős Problem 456).
%C A402157 There are 227 terms <= 10^3, 2696 terms <= 10^4, and 30525 terms <= 10^5.
%H A402157 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A402157/b402157.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A402157 Thomas F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/456">Erdős Problem 456</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A402157 Paul Erdős, <a href="https://www.numdam.org/item/AST_1979__61__73_0/">Some unconventional problems in number theory</a>, Astérisque 61 (1979), 73-82.
%e A402157 8 is a term: A061026(8) = 15 since phi(15) = 8, while A034694(8) = 17.
%e A402157 16 is not a term: A061026(16) = A034694(16) = 17.
%o A402157 (PARI) isok(k) = my(m=1); while(eulerphi(m)%k, m++); !isprime(m);
%o A402157 (Python)
%o A402157 from sympy import totient, isprime
%o A402157 def ok(k):
%o A402157     m = 1
%o A402157     while totient(m) % k: m += 1
%o A402157     return not isprime(m)
%o A402157 print([k for k in range(1, 291) if ok(k)])
%Y A402157 Cf. A000010, A034694, A061026, A398758.
%K A402157 nonn,new
%O A402157 1,2
